DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The uuffss__ddiisskk__cclloossee() function closes a disk and frees internal memory re‐
     lated to it.  It does not free the _d_i_s_k structure.

     The uuffss__ddiisskk__ffiilllloouutt() and uuffss__ddiisskk__ffiilllloouutt__bbllaannkk() functions open a disk
     specified by _n_a_m_e and populate the structure pointed to by _d_i_s_k.  The
     disk is opened read-only.  The specified _n_a_m_e may be either a mountpoint,
     a device name or a filesystem image.  The uuffss__ddiisskk__ffiilllloouutt() function as‐
     sumes there is a valid superblock and will fail if not, whereas the
     uuffss__ddiisskk__ffiilllloouutt__bbllaannkk() function makes no assumptions of that sort.

     The uuffss__ddiisskk__wwrriittee() function attempts to re-open a disk as writable if
     it is not currently.

EERRRROORRSS
     The function uuffss__ddiisskk__cclloossee() has no failure points.

     The function uuffss__ddiisskk__ffiilllloouutt() may fail for any of the reasons
     uuffss__ddiisskk__ffiilllloouutt__bbllaannkk() might, as well as for any reason sbread(3)
     might.

     The uuffss__ddiisskk__ffiilllloouutt__bbllaannkk() may fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any of the errors
     specified for the library functions open(2), strdup(3).  Additionally, it
     may follow the libufs(3) error methodologies in situations where no de‐
     vice could be found to open.

     The function uuffss__ddiisskk__wwrriittee() may fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any of the er‐
     rors specified for the library functions open(2) and stat(2).  Namely, it
     will fail if the disk in question may not be written to.

